analysisWe need to parse the comment: "@@@ If the list is not null, then this RoleUnresolvedList is equal to the list provided."

We need to produce an assert statement specification reflecting that.

We have constructor RoleUnresolvedList(List<RoleUnresolved> list). It throws IllegalArgumentException if list is null or contains non-RoleUnresolved objects. The comment says "If the list is not null, then this RoleUnresolvedList is equal to the list provided."

In spec we need to assert something after the constructor. In Java, constructors can't have a return value; but we can assert a postcondition about the object (this). So we need to assert that if the precondition list != null, then this list equals the provided list. Equality maybe defined as same elements in same order. Since we have only observer methods: size(), get(int), maybe contains, isEmpty, etc. We can express that for all indices i from 0 to list.size()-1, this.get(i).equals(list.get(i)). And also size equals list.size(). That would capture equality.

The comment says "If the list is not null, then this RoleUnresolvedList is equal to the list provided." Since the constructor throws if null, the case list null is not reachable (exception). But we can still encode as: list != null => (this.size() == list.size() && \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < list.size(); this.get(i).equals(list.get(i))).

We must ensure we use public observer methods (this.size(), this.get(i)). The list parameter is a List<RoleUnresolved>. We can use list.get(i) directly as it's a parameter (allowed). The spec may use \old? Not needed because we are after construction; we don't need old values.

Thus final assert:

assert list != null => (this.size() == list.size() && \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < list.size(); this.get(i).equals(list.get(i)));

Make sure parentheses for implication.

Return as code block.

